```python
import os
import re
import subprocess

def compile_and_test(csharp_file, test_file):
    cs_code = open(csharp_file).read()
    test_str = "<code>Debug.Assert(sum >= x && sum >= y);</code>"
    pattern = r'//\s*@@@\s*(.*?)(?=//|\s*@@@)'

    matches = re.findall(pattern, cs_code, re.DOTALL)

    test_code = open(test_file, 'w')
    test_code.write("using System.Diagnostics;\n")
    test_code.write("[Test]\n")
    test_code.write("public void TestMethod()")
    test_code.write("{\n")
    for i, match in enumerate(matches):
        test_code.write(test_str.replace("sum", match))
    test_code.write("}")

    subprocess.call(['csc.exe', '/out:test.exe', test_file])
    runner = subprocess.Popen('test.exe', shell=True, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT)
    stdout_, _ = runner.communicate()

compile_and_test("your_csharp_file.cs", "output_csharp_file.cs")
```
This function uses regex to find the comments that start with "@@@" in the C# file, then it copies them and prepares a new C# file to use assertions. It compiles the new file with csc.exe (/out:test.exe test.cs) and runs this compiled file. It will print debug outputs of running it. You may need to use a more complex approach to parse the real C# files and not just assume that the comments containing the assert conditions are there.--------------------Here's the output for the given C# code:

